WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:34.150 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  hello  my name is zizel slipovic and i'm  musician in residence at yale  university's  fortune of video archive for holocaust  testimonies  this video series is designed as a  companion to our album  where is our homeland which is the first  volume in the archives ongoing effort  to present songs and poems performed by  survivors in testimonies given to the  fortune of archive  over the last 40 years you can learn  more about the project on the archives  website  songsfromtestimonies.org  where is our homeland songs from  testimonies volume 1  was recorded in 2019 by the cecil  slepovich ensemble  featuring renowned singer sasha luria  craig udelman on the violin joshua camp  on the accordion  keyboards and guitar dmitry ischenko on  the base  and myself on woodwinds the songs in  this collection differ vastly in terms  of style  social and historical background  language and many other aspects  but collectively they create a  multi-dimensional picture  of jewish life before and during world  war  ii in this episode  we will be continuing talking about and  listening to the songs  sung by lube of k a native of the  ukrainian town of zvenigarotka  a former inmate of the slave labor camp  in the same area this musical project  began with lubbock's interview with  libov's testimony  and this song prashay  farewell is written in russian  it was remembered by lubov as a poem  that people who were who were leaving  their homes abandoning  their dear places  that they loved all their lives they  were passing this poem  to each other in this way sharing that  sorrow  and sustaining and supporting each other  i wrote music to this poem  in the genre of salon romance of the  bard  song so popular in uh in the soviet  union and  uh it came from the even earlier era  from the 19th  century uh sentimentalism  uh sentimentalist song that was very  popular in  the former russian empire an urban genre  uh that was also shared by people  and was used to convey earnest  feelings  so farewell  [Music]  be  [Music]  foreign  [Music]  foreign  scary  [Music]  [Applause]  [Music]  [Music]  [Applause]  [Music]  [Applause]  [Music]  [Music]  is  [Music]  foreign  [Music]  after launching operation barbarossa on  june 22nd  1941 the german army reached the central  ukrainian town of sinigarhka on july  29th  in 1939 the jewish population of the  city had hovered just below 2000  with jews representing approximately  fourteen percent  of the total population  que recalls that the first few days of  the german occupation were relatively  calm  however the germans soon began going  house to house to pillage  and to terrorize the local inhabitants  in september of that year the germans  forced  all jews in zvenigarka to move into a  ghetto  which occupied only two streets and one  alley  conditions in the ghetto were so crowded  that there were two to three families  living  in each house although this vinegar  coquetto was not walled off  it was closely guarded by germans and  local auxiliary police  known as the police in the ghetto young  able-bodied jews like lubov were  assigned to menial and humiliating tasks  such as cleaning latrines as they worked  non-jewish school children taunted them  by singing anti-semitic ditties and  mocking their misfortunes  on may 2 1942 the jews of nearby  al-shanna were brought to the svenigor  kagero  shortly thereafter on may 5th the  ukrainian  pulitzai and german gendarmeri rounded  up approximately 300  able-bodied jews from the ghetto and  transferred them to an  improvised labor camp in the village of  nyamrosh  which lies 8 kilometers northwest of  svinegorka  in her interview libouf recalls  quote a german came to us and said we  should take two changes of clothing  underwear outerwear and go to the jewish  council  end quote there they would await their  transfer to an uncertain future  in late september early october of the  previous year  a detachment of einsatz commando 5 had  rounded up one hundred jewish men from  the svenigor kagero  on the pretext of taking them for labor  somewhere else  instead they were shot in the laptive  meadow  this was the first mass shooting of  svenigorod could choose  so in may 1942 when new buff and  approximately 300 other jews were  rounded up to be taken for labor  they were understandably prepared for  the worst  it is this profound sense of anguishing  uncertainty  that inspired the words of their song  prashay which liborf recites  in her testimony quote farewell  or native city farewell family so dear  farewell precious mothers farewell to  all our friends  blindly we walk down the road not  knowing what lies ahead  the ominous thought is growing that  awaiting us is death  end quote as it turned out the germans  had  other plans for this group of three  hundreds finigorca  and al-shaana jews libouf and her fellow  prisoners were taken to nyamarush  where they were forced to perform  back-breaking labor on the transit  highway number four  which stretched from leve in the west to  tagan rogue  in russia in the east with tributary  roads  running north to south luboff and the  other jewish prisoners in the neymar's  camp  worked on one such tributary road which  led north to kiev  the laborers were given only the most  rudimentary of tools  shovels and picks with which they were  asked to build  maintain and repair the highway  and while the labor they provided was  valuable the german overseers and local  auxiliary police who guarded them  saw these prisoners as expendable  to say the conditions in the neymar's  camp were rustic would be a profound  understatement the prisoners lived in an  unheated barn  and as lebroff recalls quote we slept  directly  on the ground there was nothing to cover  ourselves with  so we returned from work in our clothes  and ate the thin gruel that they gave us  they cooked it for us twice a day  morning and evening  no salt a quarter of a bucket of millet  to 17 buckets of water end quote  the prisoner's daily struggle for  survival under these conditions inspired  the words of their song  quote such a home we were given a pig  stall without a window or a door  a heavy wind blew right through us and  chilled us down to the bone  at first we were not used to it such  savagery  brutality with sweat we toiled to till  the earth  and watered it with tears end quote  in the evenings after work ended new  buff and her fellow prisoners passed the  time by singing  and composing songs this process  of writing songs and sharing them  boosted their morale immeasurably as  they endured daily hardships and losses  giving voice to their feelings of hope  and despair  you

